 3. 👀 Third-party spoilers 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: FEC. (Heikes is a self-identified Republican, but Sen. Dan S. Sullivan is the Republican Party-endorsed candidate in the race.) Table: Andrew Solender/Axios More than 40 third-party candidates in pivotal House and Senate races across the country are receiving support from outside super PACs, many with ties to the two major parties, Axios' Andrew Solender reports. 

 Why it matters: The sums being thrown around, which total more than $16 million, are far greater than virtually any of those candidates have raised or spent on their own. 💰 While it's a comparative drop in the bucket for a midterm that has already seen billions in ad spending nationally, the money will likely play an outsized role in races that are set to be decided by razor-thin margins.

 Those races, in turn, will help decide the balance of power in Congress next year. Between the lines : Third-party candidates in Alaska are facing national scrutiny after The New York Times reported that Heikes and another self-proclaimed Republican on the ballot, Dan J. Sullivan, were recruited into the race.

 Republicans have long accused Dan J. Sullivan of being a spoiler meant to siphon votes from the incumbent senator by confusing voters with their shared name. 🔎 The "double-Dan" case is unique in that it reportedly included direct involvement from a political operative working with the Peltola campaign, who allegedly used pseudonyms as part of the effort.

 The Peltola campaign and the Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee have denied recruiting any third-party candidates into the race. Keep reading . 

 
 
