 6. ✊ Western despair: Youth protests spread across Europe 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 After hours of clashes between police and protesters, a group dressed in Halloween costumes gathers last night in La Plaine square in Marseille, France. Photo: Sener Yilmaz Aslan/Getty Images Hundreds of thousands of European youths took to the streets of France (for the third consecutive weekend), Germany, Portugal and Albania yesterday for anti-government protests against high living and education costs. 

 In Frankfurt, a global business hub, Gen Z-led rallies turned to riots . Over the past two weeks,  social unrest has also hit Spain and Italy, with cities turning into battlegrounds in top EU economies. Why it matters: Despair "now is the dominant mood" in the West, New York Times opinion contributor Anton Jäger writes from Brussels . The rage, not strictly left or right, is rising from "generational futility."

 🇫🇷 In France, police estimated 320,000 people, some in Halloween costumes, demonstrated nationwide yesterday, down from 550,000 and 410,000 on the previous two weekends. 

 How it started : On Oct. 17, frustrated high-school students aired grievances about dilapidated schools and uncertain futures. Widespread rioting led to a week of curfews and mass nationwide police deployment. Labor unions, radical activists and other adults joined the movement. 🇩🇪 In Frankfurt late yesterday, hooded rioters looted stores after tens of thousands of demonstrators took to the streets across Germany's biggest cities to demand better education and lower living costs. Some rioters threw Molotov cocktails at police after pummeling them with stones and bottles.

 Shops boarded up windows along Berlin's luxury Kurfürstendamm shopping boulevard. ( Reuters ) Weekend photos from across Europe. 

 
 
